-- Under the Bonnet: Going Deeper. The follow-on to Digital Foundations.
-- How code runs, tools and teams, containers, databases, graphs, race
-- conditions and AI, in plain English for non-technical project members.
--
-- Safe to run more than once: if a course with this title already exists the
-- whole script does nothing (and glossary terms use INSERT IGNORE so existing
-- definitions are never overwritten). Requires sql/schema.sql to have been run.
-- Diagrams referenced as local:*.svg live in assets/img/learning/.

SET @exists := (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM pm_learning_courses WHERE title = 'Under the Bonnet: Going Deeper');
INSERT INTO pm_learning_courses (title, description, sort_order)
SELECT 'Under the Bonnet: Going Deeper', 'The follow-on to Digital Foundations, for people who want to understand how software is really made and where it quietly goes wrong. From punched cards to quantum, memory and storage, how code runs, the languages and tools teams use, containers and DevOps, the main kinds of database and graphs, YAML and other formats, race conditions and how banks keep money safe, where complexity should live, how to get the best from AI, and encryption, cyber security and open source. Plain English, with animated diagrams and something to try in every lesson. Best read after Digital Foundations.', (SELECT COALESCE(MAX(c.sort_order), 0) + 1 FROM pm_learning_courses c)
FROM DUAL WHERE @exists = 0;
SET @course := IF(@exists = 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);

-- ===== Module 1: From Punched Cards to Quantum: How Computing Hardware Got Here =====
INSERT INTO pm_learning_modules (course_id, title, description, sort_order)
SELECT @course, 'From Punched Cards to Quantum: How Computing Hardware Got Here', 'How a card with 80 columns of holes still shapes the files and screens of today, how switches shrank from valves to transistors, how storage and memory grew, and where the next jump may come from.', 1 FROM DUAL WHERE @course > 0;
SET @mod := IF(@course > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);

INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'From weaving looms to 80-column cards: where today\'s formats come from', 'Punched cards, IBM, batch processing, and why a rectangle of card from the 1920s still influences the systems the Repairs project depends on.', '## Information on a card\n\nBefore screens, before keyboards, before files, information was held on **cards with holes in them**. The idea is older than computers. In 1804 the French weaver Joseph Marie Jacquard used chains of punched cards to tell a loom which threads to lift, so one machine could weave a complex pattern again and again. A hole meant "yes, lift this thread". No hole meant "no". That is information stored as a pattern of on and off.\n\nIn the 1880s an American, **Herman Hollerith**, applied the same idea to counting people. His machines read cards by pushing pins through the holes to complete an electrical circuit, and they tabulated the 1890 US census far faster than hand counting. His company later became part of the firm renamed **IBM** in 1924. By then punched cards were the backbone of business data processing: payroll, accounts, stock, and local authority records.\n\n## The 80-column card\n\nIn 1928 IBM introduced the card that became the world standard: **80 columns** with **12 positions** in each. A column of holes is one character. A hole in a single position is a digit, and a combination of two holes makes a letter.\n\n![A punched card with the words REPAIR 4821 BOILER encoded as holes, scanned column by column](local:punched-card.svg)\n\nSo one card held **80 characters**: roughly one short line of text, or one record such as a name, an address and an amount. A box of 2,000 cards held about 160,000 characters. Programs were punched on cards too, one line of code per card, and a program was literally a **deck** of cards. Drop the deck and the program was in the wrong order, which is why cards often carried the warning "Do not fold, spindle or mutilate", and why many had a sequence number punched in the last columns.\n\n## A mind-set that survived the machines\n\nThe card disappeared. The way of thinking it created did not:\n\n- **Fixed-width fields.** On a card, "columns 1 to 20 are the name, 21 to 30 the postcode" was physical reality. Many systems still exchange **fixed-width files**, where each piece of information must be an exact length and position. It is why an old system may chop a long address or description at a set number of characters.\n- **80 characters per line.** Early terminals copied the card and showed **80 columns** across (often 24 rows). Programmers\' style guides, printers, email and many text tools still default to about 80 characters per line.\n- **Batch processing.** Cards were collected into a pile, fed in together and processed in one long run, often overnight, with results printed in the morning. Plenty of finance, payroll and housing systems still work this way: a **batch job** runs at night, and what you see on screen today is yesterday\'s data.\n- **The mainframe.** The large central computers built to read cards grew into the **mainframes** that, in a modern form, still run much of banking and government.\n- **Records, fields and keys.** The idea that data is a list of identical records, each made of labelled fields with a unique reference number, is the ancestor of today\'s database tables.\n\n::: remember\nOld formats outlive the technology that created them. When a modern system has a strange limit or an overnight delay, there is often a punched-card reason hiding behind it.\n:::\n\n## Why this matters to the Repairs project\n\nWhen a supplier says a system "exports a nightly file to finance", ask what that file looks like. A fixed-width or batch design from a different era can mean limits on description length, no live updates, and difficulty adding a new field. It also tells you why modern **APIs**, which move small pieces of information as they change, are such a step forward.\n\n::: try\nSearch the web for "punched card decoder" or "IBM 80 column card emulator". Type your name or "REPAIR 4821" and see which holes it makes. Then look at the printed text along the top of the card: the card printed its own contents so a person could read it without a machine. Next, open a plain text file in any editor and look at the width: how many characters fit on a line in a coding editor? Notice how close it is to 80.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nThink of one report, export or screen in the Repairs project that is only updated overnight, or truncates long text. Write down what you think its design inherits from the days of cards and batch runs, and what a live alternative would change for the people who use it.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
