INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Switches get smaller: valves, transistors and chips', 'The same job, switching on and off, done in smaller, faster and cheaper ways, and how that made the computers we use possible.', '## Every computer is switches\n\nStrip away the screens and software, and a computer is a huge number of tiny **switches** that can be on or off, joined so that their on and off states represent numbers, letters and decisions. A punched card did exactly that with holes. The history of computing hardware is the history of finding better switches.\n\n- **Relays** were mechanical switches moved by electromagnets, clicking like a very busy typewriter. They were slow and wore out.\n- **Valves** (vacuum tubes) switched electricity inside glass bulbs with no moving parts, which made them thousands of times faster. But they were large, hot, power-hungry and burned out. The ENIAC of 1945 used about 17,000 of them, filled a large room and weighed over 20 tonnes.\n- **Transistors**, invented at Bell Labs in 1947, did the same job in a tiny piece of solid material, with no glass, no filament, and almost no heat by comparison. The punched-card world\'s machines became smaller, cheaper and reliable because of them.\n- **Integrated circuits** (1958 and 1959) put many transistors, joined together, on one slice of silicon. Then the **microprocessor** (Intel\'s 4004 in 1971) put a whole processor on a single chip, with about 2,300 transistors.\n\nThe important idea is that nothing about the **task** changed. A switch is still a switch. What changed was the **physical form** of the switch, and each new form was smaller, faster, cheaper and more reliable than the last. The computer that filled a room in the card era now fits in a pocket.\n\n## Moore\'s law\n\nIn 1965 Gordon Moore, who later co-founded Intel, noticed that the number of components on a chip was doubling at a steady rate, and later put it at roughly **every two years**. This became known as **Moore\'s law**. It is not a law of nature. It is an observation that became a target the whole industry worked towards.\n\n![Transistors per chip from 1971 to today, doubling about every two years](local:moores-law.svg)\n\nDoubling about every two years sounds mild until it is repeated. Fifty years is 25 doublings, which is more than a **thirty-million-fold** increase. The chip in a phone today holds **billions** of transistors. This is why a modern phone is more powerful than the room-sized machines of the card era, and why, for decades, software could assume next year\'s hardware would be much faster and cheaper.\n\n## What "nanometre" means\n\nYou will hear chips described in **nanometres** (nm). A nanometre is one **billionth of a metre**. A human hair is about 80,000 nm wide, and a silicon atom is about a fifth of a nanometre across. Modern manufacturing names (such as "3 nm") are partly marketing labels and no longer measure one exact feature, but the point stands: the parts are now only a few dozen atoms wide.\n\n## Why the Repairs project should care\n\n- **Cheap, fast hardware is why digital services are possible.** Things that were once unaffordable, such as photos from every phone, instant maps and live tracking, exist because of this long shrinking.\n- **The era of free speed-ups has slowed.** A later lesson in this module sets out why. It means well-written, efficient software now matters more.\n- **Each new form has been a re-invention, not an upgrade.** Cards, valves and transistors are not versions of one thing. A new approach arrives, does the same job differently, and eventually replaces the old one. Keep this in mind when you consider cloud, AI or quantum.\n\n::: try\nLook up a photo of **ENIAC** and of the chip in your own phone (search "ENIAC photograph" and "A-series chip die photo"). Then compare numbers: ENIAC had about 17,000 valves; a modern phone chip has over ten billion transistors. How many ENIACs would it take to match the phone\'s switches, ignoring size? (Divide 10,000,000,000 by 17,000.) The answer is about 590,000.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nSame job, new physical form: relay, valve, transistor, chip. The punched card was one more step in a long search for a better switch.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
