INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Storage through the ages: from cards to floppies to the cloud', 'How much each generation of storage held, why old media becomes unreadable, and what that means for keeping records safe.', '## How much is a punched card?\n\nLet us use the punched card as a ruler. One card holds 80 characters, about 80 **bytes**. A **byte** is a unit big enough to hold one character, and every other size is built from it: a **kilobyte (KB)** is about a thousand bytes, a **megabyte (MB)** a million, a **gigabyte (GB)** a billion and a **terabyte (TB)** a trillion.\n\n![How many punched cards fit on a floppy disk, CD, DVD and 1 TB drive](local:storage-ladder.svg)\n\nWatch the diagram build. A **floppy disk** holds the equivalent of about 18,000 cards. A **CD** holds about 8.75 million. A single terabyte drive, which fits in a pocket, holds the equivalent of 12.5 **billion** cards, a stack roughly 2,250 km tall. Each generation did the same job, keeping information safe until needed, but in a smaller space.\n\n## The family tree\n\n- **Punched cards and paper tape** (to the 1960s): one pile of cards per job.\n- **Magnetic tape** (from the 1950s): reels that held thousands of cards\' worth of data, read in sequence. Tape is still used today for **deep archives**, because it is cheap and lasts.\n- **Hard disks** (IBM\'s RAMAC, 1956): spinning platters read by a moving arm. The first held about five million characters, filled a cabinet and weighed over a tonne. A modern disk holds millions of times more.\n- **Floppy disks** (IBM introduced them in 1971): small, portable, rewritable. Famously, the 3.5-inch version held 1.44 MB.\n- **Optical discs** (CD, DVD, Blu-ray): data stored as tiny pits read by a laser.\n- **Flash memory** (memory sticks, phones, SSDs): no moving parts, data held as electrical charge.\n- **The cloud:** not a new medium but a service. Your files sit on somebody else\'s disks, in a data centre, copied to several places.\n\n## Problems that come with storage\n\nStoring information is easy. **Keeping it usable** is the hard part.\n\n- **Formats and readers disappear.** A floppy is useless without a floppy drive. A file saved in a long-dead program may be unreadable even if the disk is fine. This is called **format obsolescence**, and archivists talk of a "digital dark age".\n- **Media decay.** Discs delaminate, tape stretches, magnetic charge fades, flash cells wear. Data slowly corrupts, sometimes called **bit rot**. Recordable CDs from the 1990s are already failing.\n- **Backups.** Disks die. A widely used rule of thumb is **3-2-1**: three copies, on two different kinds of media, with one stored somewhere else. For ransomware protection, at least one copy should be offline or unchangeable.\n- **Cost, speed and capacity pull against each other.** Fast storage (SSD) costs more per gigabyte than slow (tape). Organisations use a mix: fast for what is in daily use, cheap for what must only be kept.\n- **The cloud is not magic.** Someone must pay for it every month, getting data back out can cost money, and where the data physically sits has legal consequences.\n- **Volume.** Photos, scans, sensor data and email pile up. Without rules about what to keep and for how long, storage costs and risk both grow.\n\n## Records and the law\n\nPublic bodies must keep records properly. In Scotland, the **Public Records (Scotland) Act 2011** requires authorities to have a **records management plan** describing how they create, keep, protect and dispose of records. A repair record may need to be kept for many years, for example for safety certificates or disputes. That raises a practical question: **how will it still be readable in 15 years**, long after the current system, supplier and file format have gone?\n\n::: try\nDo the arithmetic yourself. A megabyte is a million bytes and a card holds 80. Divide 1,440,000 (a floppy) by 80 and get 18,000 cards. Now do the same for your phone: look in its settings for how much storage it has (for example 128 GB, which is 128 billion bytes). How many cards is that? Then find the oldest digital file you own and ask: could I open it today, and on what?\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nPick one record in the Repairs project that must be kept for a long time, such as a gas safety certificate. Describe where it is stored now, in what file format, how many copies exist, and what would let someone read it in 15 years.\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
