INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'RAM, ROM and storage: why memory is not the same as storage', 'The difference between the working desk, the filing cabinet and the start-up instructions, and the problems each one causes.', '## Three kinds of "memory"\n\nPeople say "memory" for several different things. Learning to tell them apart explains a lot of everyday computer behaviour.\n\n![Cache, RAM, storage and ROM compared, with what happens when the power is cut](local:memory-hierarchy.svg)\n\n**RAM (Random Access Memory).** The computer\'s **working desk**. When you open a file or run a program, the computer copies what it needs into RAM, because RAM is **very fast**. It is also **volatile**: it forgets everything the moment the power goes off. "Random access" means any piece can be reached equally quickly, not only in order.\n\n**Storage (SSD or hard disk).** The **filing cabinet**. It holds your files and programs permanently, even with the power off. It is much **bigger and cheaper** per gigabyte than RAM, but **slower**.\n\n**ROM (Read-Only Memory).** A small amount of memory that **keeps its contents without power** and holds the instructions a device needs to **start up**, called **firmware** (the BIOS or UEFI on a PC). When you press the power button, the processor has nothing in RAM yet, so it begins by running instructions held in ROM, which then load the operating system from storage into RAM. Despite the name, modern "ROM" is usually **flash memory** that can be updated carefully, which is what a firmware update is.\n\nBetween the processor and RAM sits **cache**, a tiny amount of even faster memory that keeps what the processor is using most often. This is the same idea as the **caching** you will meet later in this course: keep recent answers close to where they are needed.\n\n## A useful picture\n\nThink of a planner working at a desk. The **filing cabinet** holds every repair file, but the planner takes the files being worked on today and lays them on the **desk** (RAM). The desk is small, but everything on it is instantly reachable. If the planner leaves at the end of the day and clears the desk, only what was put back in the cabinet (saved) is kept. The **laminated instructions on the wall** about how to open the office are the ROM.\n\n## Everyday problems this explains\n\n- **"It slows down when I have lots of things open."** RAM is full, so the computer shuffles data to and from the much slower storage (called **swapping** or **paging**), and everything crawls.\n- **"Turning it off and on again fixed it."** A restart empties RAM, clearing a build-up of mistakes such as a **memory leak**, where a program keeps grabbing memory and never gives it back.\n- **"I lost my unsaved work."** It was only ever in RAM.\n- **"Out of memory" versus "disk full".** Different shortages, different fixes: one needs more RAM or fewer running programs, the other more storage or tidying.\n- **Why SSDs transformed old laptops.** Swapping storage for a much faster type made starting up and opening files feel quick, without touching the processor.\n- **Bottlenecks.** A fast processor can be left waiting on slower memory or storage. Modern computer design is largely about feeding the processor quickly enough.\n- **Firmware flaws.** Because ROM-style firmware runs before everything else, mistakes or attacks there are hard to detect and repair. Updating firmware is part of security.\n\n## Servers and the cloud\n\nThe same ideas apply on a large scale. A database server often performs best when most of its working data fits in RAM. Systems that must not lose information keep writing important changes to storage immediately, so a power cut does not lose them. Cloud suppliers sell RAM, processor and storage separately, which is why a service\'s bill and its speed depend on getting that balance right.\n\n::: try\nOpen your computer\'s activity monitor: **Task Manager** (Ctrl+Shift+Esc on Windows, then the Performance tab) or **Activity Monitor** on a Mac (Memory tab). Find how much RAM you have and how much is in use. Open ten browser tabs and watch the number change. Then find how much storage is used. You are looking at the desk and the filing cabinet side by side.\n:::\n\n::: remember\n**RAM** = fast, working, forgets when switched off. **Storage** = slower, big, remembers. **ROM / firmware** = the start-up instructions that remember too.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nA planner\'s screen froze and the half-typed note was lost. In plain English, explain what happened to that note in terms of RAM and storage, and name one design feature that would have prevented the loss (for example saving a draft automatically as the person types).\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
