  UNION ALL SELECT 'The screen is too small' AS t, 0 AS c, 'Those have no effect on speed. Running out of RAM does.' AS e, 2 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'The keyboard has too many keys' AS t, 0 AS c, 'Those have no effect on speed. Running out of RAM does.' AS e, 3 AS s) o WHERE @q > 0;

INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'The next jump: smaller, stranger and quantum', 'Why Moore\'s law is slowing, the ideas that may carry computing forward, and a plain-English look at what quantum computers can and cannot do.', '## The end of easy\n\nFor decades, shrinking transistors also made chips faster and cooler, so each year\'s computers were simply better. Around the mid-2000s the free ride ended. Chips could no longer run much faster without overheating, and the parts are now only a few dozen atoms wide. Smaller parts also leak, costs have soared (a modern chip factory costs tens of billions), and only a handful of companies in the world can make the most advanced chips.\n\nProgress has not stopped. It has changed shape.\n\n- **More cores.** Instead of one faster processor, put many side by side. This helps only if the software can split its work to use them (which links to the concurrency problems later in this course).\n- **Specialised chips.** Chips designed for one kind of job do it far more efficiently than a general one. **Graphics chips (GPUs)** and AI accelerators are the best known examples, and they are why modern AI became possible.\n- **Building upwards and in pieces.** Stacking layers of circuits and combining small chips ("chiplets") in one package.\n- **Better software.** When hardware stops doing the work for free, efficient code matters again. There is an old saying, **Wirth\'s law**: software gets slower faster than hardware gets faster.\n\n## Smaller still: nanotechnology and new materials\n\n**Nanotechnology** means engineering at the scale of billionths of a metre, and it is already in every modern chip. Research looks for new materials such as **graphene** and **carbon nanotubes**, new ways of moving signals with light instead of electricity (**photonics**), and chips inspired by the brain (**neuromorphic** computing) that use far less energy. Some of these will be important, and some will fade, as relays and valves once did.\n\nStorage is changing too. Researchers have stored data in **DNA**, which in principle can hold enormous amounts in a tiny space for thousands of years, but it is currently slow and very expensive, so it remains experimental.\n\n## Quantum computing, in plain English\n\nA normal computer bit is a switch that is **either 0 or 1**. A **quantum computer** uses **qubits**, built from extremely small things such as atoms or electrons, which obey the strange rules of quantum physics. Two ideas matter:\n\n- **Superposition.** A qubit can be in a blend of 0 and 1 until it is measured, so a group of qubits can represent many possibilities at once.\n- **Entanglement.** Qubits can be linked so that what happens to one is tied to the others.\n\nCleverly designed procedures use these effects so that wrong answers cancel out and right answers are reinforced. This does **not** mean a quantum computer simply tries everything at once and picks the winner.\n\n**What it is good for:** a small set of problems, such as simulating molecules and materials (useful for medicines and batteries), some kinds of optimisation, and **breaking certain encryption** (more below).\n\n**What it is not:** a faster everything. It will not run a spreadsheet, a website or a repairs system faster. Quantum machines are also delicate. Today\'s are small, noisy, kept near absolute zero, and make a lot of mistakes, so real, large-scale, useful machines are still some way off, and predictions differ about how far.\n\n## Why quantum matters to security today\n\nMuch of the encryption that protects the internet depends on mathematical problems that are too hard for today\'s computers. A large, reliable quantum computer could solve some of them. Nobody can break today\'s encryption this way yet, but some attackers are already **harvesting** encrypted data now to decrypt later ("harvest now, decrypt later"). So standards bodies have developed new **post-quantum cryptography**. In 2024 the US standards body NIST published its first post-quantum standards, and the UK\'s National Cyber Security Centre has published a timeline for organisations to move over. A later module explains encryption itself. For the Repairs project, the practical point is simple: ask suppliers whether they have a plan for it, especially for data that must stay confidential for many years.\n\n## The pattern repeats\n\nCards, valves, transistors, chips, and perhaps one day quantum: each time, people found a different physical way to do a similar job, and the older ways were slowly replaced. What stays constant is the **information**, and the need to design systems so that data can move from one generation of technology to the next. That is why open formats, good documentation and clear exit plans matter so much.\n\n::: try\nSearch for "IBM Quantum" or "Quantum Composer" and open the free online simulator or the "learn" pages. Build a tiny circuit with one qubit, apply the "H" (Hadamard) gate and run it many times. You will see the results come out roughly half 0 and half 1: superposition, simulated on an ordinary computer. Notice how small the circuits are compared with anything a business uses.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nList one thing in the Repairs project that must stay readable and confidential for more than 15 years. Write one question for the supplier about how it will survive both a change of technology and the arrival of stronger code-breaking.\n:::', 5, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
