INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Frameworks, React and ready-made building blocks', 'Libraries versus frameworks, why almost nobody starts from a blank page, and what React is.', '## Nobody starts from nothing\n\nImagine building a house. You would not mix your own concrete or forge your own nails. You would buy bricks, windows and standard fittings and spend your time on the *design*. Software works the same way. Developers rely on huge amounts of ready-made code written by others, so that they can focus on what is special about the job.\n\n## Library or framework?\n\nThese two words are often confused.\n\n**Library.** A collection of ready-made code for one particular job (handling dates, drawing charts, sending email). **You** are in charge and call the library when you need it. Think of a toolbox.\n\n**Framework.** A ready-made structure for a whole kind of application. It decides how your code is organised and **calls your code** at the right moments. Think of a prefabricated building frame: you fit your rooms inside it. Frameworks give speed and consistency, at the price of doing things their way.\n\n## What is React?\n\n**React** is a hugely popular JavaScript library (often treated as a framework) for building user interfaces. Its big idea is the **component**: a small, reusable piece of screen, such as a "repair card", a "date picker" or a "status badge". Screens are assembled from components, like building with standard bricks.\n\nWhy it became so popular:\n\n- **Reuse.** Build the status badge once, use it on fifty screens.\n- **Consistency.** Fix it in one place, it is fixed everywhere.\n- **Describe, don\'t micro-manage.** You say what the screen should look like for the current data, and React works out the minimal changes to make to the DOM (from the previous lesson).\n\nOther well-known names you will hear: **Angular** and **Vue** (alternatives to React for the front end), **Next.js** (adds server features on top of React), **Laravel**, **Django** and **ASP.NET** (back-end frameworks in PHP, Python and C#).\n\n## The trade-offs\n\n- **Dependencies.** A modern project may depend on hundreds of other packages. Each needs updating, and a vulnerability in any one can affect you. This is called the **supply chain**, and it is why security teams ask for a list of components.\n- **Churn.** Frameworks rise and fade. Choosing a widely used one reduces the risk of being stranded.\n- **Lock-in.** Heavy use of a framework\'s special features makes it hard to move later.\n\n::: try\nVisit a site you use often and open the developer tools (**F12**). In the **Network** tab, reload and look at the files loaded. Names containing `react`, `vue`, `angular` or `next` hint at what it is built with. You can also install the free **Wappalyzer** browser extension, which lists the technologies a site uses. Try it on three different sites, including a council site.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nA supplier says their repairs portal is "built on a modern React front end with a documented API". In plain English, what does that tell you, and which of the lock-in questions from the supplier lesson would you ask next?\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
