SELECT @mod, 'Developer tools: IDEs, autocomplete, linting and Prettier', 'The everyday helpers that stop mistakes before they reach users, and why these quiet habits matter to quality.', '## Inside a developer workshop\n\nWriting software is detailed work where a missing bracket can break a system. So developers surround themselves with tools that catch mistakes early, when they are cheap to fix. You do not need to use these tools, but knowing they exist helps you judge whether a supplier is working professionally.\n\n## The IDE: the editor with a brain\n\nAn **IDE (Integrated Development Environment)** is the program a developer writes code in. It is far more than a text editor. Popular ones include **Visual Studio Code**, **Visual Studio**, and the **JetBrains** family. Typical abilities:\n\n- **Syntax highlighting:** colours different parts of the code so the structure is clear.\n- **Autocomplete:** suggests the rest of what is being typed, like predictive text on a phone, but aware of the whole project. Modern AI assistants take this much further, suggesting entire blocks of code.\n- **Go to definition:** jump straight to where something is defined.\n- **Debugger:** pause a running program and look at its information step by step to find why it misbehaves.\n- **Built-in terminal, Git and testing.**\n\n## Linting: a spell-checker for code\n\nA **linter** automatically scans code for likely mistakes and bad habits: a variable that is never used, a comparison that is almost certainly a typo, a risky pattern. Like a spell-checker, it underlines problems as you type. A well known one for JavaScript is **ESLint**. Linters also enforce a team\'s agreed rules.\n\n## Formatting: ending the arguments\n\nA **formatter** such as **Prettier** automatically rewrites code into one consistent layout each time it is saved: spacing, line breaks, quote marks. Why does it matter?\n\n- Reviewers can focus on **what the code does**, not on arguments about spacing.\n- Changes show up clearly in version control because only real edits differ.\n- Anyone can read anyone else\'s code.\n\n## Tests and the pipeline\n\nLinting and formatting catch style and likely errors. **Automated tests** check behaviour: "when a repair is marked urgent, the target date is four hours ahead". These run automatically every time code changes, as part of the CI/CD pipeline from the first course. A team with automated tests can change things confidently. A team without them tends to be frightened of change, which is slow and risky.\n\n## A quick quality check on a supplier\n\nAsk: *Do you use linting, formatting and automated tests? What proportion of the code is covered by tests? Is it all run automatically before release?* Teams who care will answer instantly and proudly.\n\n::: try\nInstall the free **Visual Studio Code** (code.visualstudio.com) and make a new file called `hello.js`. Type `console.log("Hello` and watch it suggest and auto-close the quote and bracket. Delete the closing bracket on purpose and see the red wavy underline appear: that is the editor and linter noticing a mistake before the code was ever run. Then paste some untidy code and use **Format Document** to see it tidy itself.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nThese tools are about **catching problems early and cheaply**. The earlier a mistake is found, the less it costs. A mistake found by a linter costs seconds; the same mistake found by a tenant costs trust.\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
