INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Containers, Docker and Kubernetes', 'How software is packaged so that it runs the same everywhere, and how thousands of packages are kept running automatically.', '## "It works on my machine"\n\nSoftware depends on its surroundings: the right operating system, the right language version, the right libraries, the right settings. A tiny difference between a developer\'s laptop and the live server can make it fail. This is one of the oldest frustrations in IT.\n\n## Containers: a sealed lunchbox\n\nA **container** packages an application together with **everything it needs** to run: its code, libraries and settings. Wherever the container is started, it behaves the same way, like a sealed lunchbox that holds everything for the meal no matter which table you sit at.\n\n**Docker** is the tool that made containers mainstream. A developer describes the package in a small file, builds an **image** (the template) and then starts **containers** from it (the running copies).\n\n### Containers versus virtual machines\n\nYou may also hear about **virtual machines (VMs)**, which came first. A VM imitates an entire computer, including its own full operating system. A container shares the host\'s operating system and carries only the application and what it needs.\n\n![A virtual machine carries a full guest operating system; a container shares the host and is far lighter](local:containers-vs-vms.svg)\n\nThe result: containers are **small, start in seconds, and many can run on one machine**. VMs are heavier but offer stronger separation. Both are widely used, often together.\n\n## Kubernetes: the container manager\n\nRunning one container is easy. Running hundreds across many servers, keeping the right number alive, updating them without downtime and spreading the load is not. **Kubernetes** (often written **K8s**) automates this. You tell it the **desired state** ("always run three copies of the repairs API"), and it works constantly to make reality match.\n\n![Kubernetes notices a failed container and replaces it automatically to keep the desired number running](local:kubernetes-heals.svg)\n\nWhat it gives:\n\n- **Self-healing:** if a container crashes, a replacement starts.\n- **Scaling:** add more copies when demand rises (a cold snap and a flood of heating repairs), remove them afterwards.\n- **Rolling updates:** replace old versions gradually with no downtime, and roll back if something looks wrong.\n- **Declarative configuration:** the desired state is written in **YAML** files kept in version control.\n\n## What you actually need to know\n\n- Most suppliers\' cloud services already run in containers on your behalf. You are unlikely to manage Kubernetes yourself.\n- It is a strong sign of a modern, repeatable, scalable delivery approach, **but also of complexity**. Kubernetes is powerful and demands skilled people. For a small system it can be overkill.\n- Containers make the "same in test as in live" promise real, supporting the environments and UAT from the first course.\n\n::: try\nSearch the web for **"Play with Docker"** (an online practice site run by Docker) and follow its first lab in your browser. You do not need to install anything. Start a container and see it respond. Notice how quickly it starts compared with anything you associate with "setting up a server".\n:::\n\n::: remember\nContainer = the **package**. Docker = the **tool that builds and runs** packages. Kubernetes = the **manager** that keeps lots of them running and healthy.\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
