INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Database types: choosing the right tool for the data', 'Relational, document, key-value, graph and time-series databases, with what each is best at.', '## One size does not fit all\n\nIn the first course you met the **relational database**: tables of rows and columns linked by keys, queried with SQL. It is the workhorse for most business systems and for good reason. But it is not the only design. Over the last twenty years, other kinds were created for jobs the table does less well. Knowing the families helps you understand suppliers\' choices.\n\n![Five database families side by side with the kind of job each is best at](local:database-types.svg)\n\n## The main families\n\n**Relational (SQL).** Data in tables with strict structure and relationships between them. Best for: transactions, records that must be accurate and consistent, anything you want to count, join and report on. Examples: **MySQL, PostgreSQL, Microsoft SQL Server, Oracle**. A repairs system\'s core records almost certainly live here.\n\n**Document.** Each record is a flexible, self-describing **document**, usually JSON, and records in the same collection can have different shapes. Best for: content, catalogues, data whose shape changes often or varies a lot between items. Examples: **MongoDB, Couchbase**. Think of a filing cabinet of folders, each holding whatever its owner put in.\n\n**Key-value.** The simplest design: store a value under a name (the **key**) and fetch it back by that name, extremely fast. Best for: sessions (the "who is logged in" information), caches, counters, queues. Examples: **Redis, Memcached**. Think of a cloakroom: hand in a ticket, get your coat.\n\n**Graph.** Stores things and the **relationships** between them as first-class parts, so questions about connections are quick: "who is connected to whom, through what, within three steps?" Best for: networks, dependency maps, recommendations, fraud detection. Examples: **Neo4j, Amazon Neptune**. You will see more in the next lesson.\n\n**Time-series.** Built for values recorded over time, such as sensor readings, and for questions about trends and averages. Best for: monitoring, internet-of-things devices, energy and environmental data (for example damp and temperature sensors in homes). Examples: **InfluxDB, TimescaleDB**.\n\nThere are also **search engines** (such as Elasticsearch) that make huge amounts of text searchable instantly, and **data warehouses** (from the first course) built for analysis rather than day-to-day transactions.\n\n## The software behind the label: the DBMS\n\nA "database" has two parts people often blur together: the **data** itself, and the **database management system (DBMS)**, the software that stores it, protects it, runs the queries and decides how to do the work. The families above are *designs*. A DBMS is a particular *product* that implements a design, and **products differ a great deal, even within one family**. Two products can both be "relational" or both be "document" and still behave, cost and fail very differently.\n\nWhat differs between products:\n\n- **The query language.** Almost every relational product speaks SQL, but each adds its own dialect (Microsoft\'s T-SQL, Oracle\'s PL/SQL). Other designs have their own languages: **Cypher** for Neo4j, **SurrealQL** for SurrealDB, and MongoDB\'s own query and aggregation syntax. A skill or a piece of code written for one does not simply move to another.\n- **The guarantees.** Some products give strict all-or-nothing transactions everywhere (the next module). Others trade some strictness for speed or for spreading data across the world, and let you choose how fresh a read must be.\n- **How it is run and paid for.** Self-installed on your own servers, or a **managed service** where a supplier runs it for you and charges by use.\n- **Licensing and lock-in.** Open source (PostgreSQL, MySQL) versus commercial (Oracle, SQL Server) versus cloud-only products.\n\n### A few products, and why they feel so different\n\n- **Microsoft SQL Server and Oracle Database.** Long-established, commercial **relational** systems common in councils and large organisations. Powerful, with rich built-in features such as stored procedures, reporting and security tooling. The price is licence cost and a strong pull towards their own dialects and features, which is the lock-in risk you will meet again in the last lesson of this module.\n- **MySQL and PostgreSQL.** Open-source relational systems. Both are widely used on the web (this learning site uses MySQL). PostgreSQL is known for rigour and rich features.\n- **MongoDB, and Atlas.** MongoDB is a **document** DBMS. **Atlas** is MongoDB\'s **managed cloud service**: a supplier runs MongoDB for you, with backups, scaling and security handled. It is a useful reminder that "which DBMS" and "who runs it" are two separate decisions.\n- **Neo4j.** A **graph** DBMS built around nodes and relationships, queried with Cypher, which reads almost like drawing the pattern you are looking for.\n- **Azure Cosmos DB.** Microsoft\'s globally distributed cloud database. It is **multi-model**: one service that can behave as a document store or a graph and can even present itself as MongoDB, Cassandra or other interfaces. Its distinctive features are spreading data across regions and offering a menu of **consistency levels**, so you choose between "always the freshest" and "faster but maybe slightly behind". It is priced by the capacity you reserve and use.\n- **SurrealDB.** A newer **multi-model** DBMS that combines document, graph and relational ideas in one product, with its own language, SurrealQL. Records can link directly to each other without the joins SQL needs, and it can push live changes out to connected screens. It can also check *who may see what* inside the database, which lets some applications talk to it more directly. That is very different from the traditional pattern (screen, then back end, then database), and from a hosted MongoDB, which is a document store behind a conventional back end.\n\nNone of these is "the right one". The lesson is that **choosing a database product shapes the whole architecture**: how the application is built, what skills are needed, how data is protected and what it costs to leave. This is why supplier questions should always include **which DBMS, on what terms, run by whom, and how do we get our data out?**\n\n## "NoSQL"\n\nYou will hear **NoSQL** used for anything that is not a traditional relational database. It is a label for what they are *not*, and mixes very different designs. It is more useful to ask which family is meant.\n\n## Choosing: the questions to ask\n\n- **How important is strict accuracy?** If money, legal records or safety are involved, a relational database with transactions (covered in the next module) is normally the right core.\n- **How regular is the data?** Fixed, well-understood shapes suit tables; wildly varied shapes suit documents.\n- **What will you ask of it?** Many connected questions suit graphs; "latest value for this key" suits key-value; trends over time suit time-series.\n- **How fast and how big?** Some designs shine at enormous scale or speed, but at the cost of strict guarantees.\n\nReal systems often use **several**: a relational database as the trusted record, a key-value store for speed, a search engine for finding things, and a warehouse for reporting. This is called **polyglot persistence**, which simply means "using the right store for each job".\n\n::: try\nSearch the web for an online "MongoDB playground" and paste in `{"address": "12 High St", "priority": "urgent", "photos": [1, 2]}`, then a second document with different fields. Notice the database accepts both. Then think how a relational table would handle a repair that suddenly needed an extra "scaffolding" field.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nThe core record of a repair is best in a relational database. The supporting jobs (speed, search, sensors, connections) are often best handled by other kinds. Neither is "better" overall.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
