INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Graphs and Microsoft Graph: connections as data', 'What a "graph" means in computing, why it fits connected information, and what Microsoft Graph is.', '## Not a chart: a network\n\nWhen people hear "graph" they think of a bar chart. In computing, a **graph** is something different: a collection of **nodes** (things) joined by **edges** (relationships). Nodes are people, properties, contractors or repairs; edges say how they relate: *lives at*, *reported*, *assigned to*, *works for*.\n\n![A graph of a tenant, property, repair, contractor and operative joined by labelled relationships](local:graph-model.svg)\n\nGraphs are natural because the real world is a network. A tenant lives in a property, which has a boiler, which was serviced by a contractor, whose operative is accredited for gas work. A table can store this, but answering "which tenants live in properties serviced by operatives whose accreditation expires this month?" requires joining several tables. A graph makes following the connections the *easy, fast, natural* operation.\n\n## Where graphs shine\n\n- **Dependency maps:** which systems depend on which, and what breaks if one fails (a "system map" is a graph).\n- **Relationships and networks:** organisations, people, supply chains.\n- **Fraud and anomaly detection:** spotting linked accounts or unusual patterns.\n- **Recommendations and search:** "people who did X also did Y".\n- **Knowledge graphs:** structured facts about the world, which also help AI systems answer accurately.\n\n## Microsoft Graph: the same idea, applied to Microsoft 365\n\n**Microsoft Graph** is Microsoft\'s **API** to the information in Microsoft 365: users, groups, mail, calendars, Teams chats, files in SharePoint and OneDrive, tasks, and more. The name reflects the idea that all these things (people, their meetings, documents, conversations) are *connected*, and the API lets software follow those connections.\n\nMany councils use Microsoft 365, so Microsoft Graph matters to them:\n\n- **One doorway.** Instead of a different API for each Microsoft product, there is one endpoint family, `https://graph.microsoft.com`.\n- **Same ideas as any API.** You use `GET` to read (a user\'s calendar), `POST` to create (send a Teams message or create an event), `PATCH` to update, and `DELETE` to remove. Responses are JSON. Authentication uses **OAuth** and **permissions** (called scopes) that an administrator must approve. All the concepts from the first course apply.\n- **Typical uses on a project:** create calendar bookings for site visits, send notifications through Teams or email, store documents in SharePoint, read staff details to assign work, or sign users in with their council accounts (single sign-on).\n- **Governance matters.** The permissions an app requests decide how much of an organisation\'s data it can reach. Apps should ask for the **least privilege** they need, and administrators should review them. Over-broad permissions are a real security risk.\n\n::: try\nOpen **Graph Explorer** (search the web for "Microsoft Graph Explorer"). It lets you try the API in your browser using sample data without signing in. Run the default query `GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me` and read the JSON that comes back. Try changing it to `.../me/messages` or `.../users`. You are using a real, enormous API with exactly the methods, status codes and JSON you learned about.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nImagine automating contractor site-visit bookings through calendars. Name two Microsoft Graph permissions you would want a security team to examine closely before approving, and say why.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
