INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'YAML and other data formats you will meet', 'JSON, YAML, XML and CSV: how to recognise each and where they are used, especially for configuration.', '## Same facts, different clothes\n\nInformation has to be written down in some agreed layout before systems can exchange it. You have met **JSON**. Here are the four formats you will actually run into, shown for the same simple repair.\n\n**JSON** (used by most APIs):\n\n```\n{\n  "ref": 4821,\n  "priority": "urgent",\n  "photos": ["leak1.jpg", "leak2.jpg"]\n}\n```\n\n**YAML** (used for configuration):\n\n```\nref: 4821\npriority: urgent\nphotos:\n  - leak1.jpg\n  - leak2.jpg\n```\n\n**XML** (older and wordier; still common in government and legacy systems):\n\n```\n<repair>\n  <ref>4821</ref>\n  <priority>urgent</priority>\n</repair>\n```\n\n**CSV** (flat tables, usable in Excel):\n\n```\nref,priority,photos\n4821,urgent,2\n```\n\n## YAML: configuration that humans can read\n\n**YAML** is designed to be easy for people to read and write. It uses **indentation** (spaces) to show structure, a `name: value` pattern, and dashes for lists. It is the language in which much of modern infrastructure is described:\n\n- **Kubernetes** and container setups: "run three copies of this".\n- **CI/CD pipelines:** "when code is pushed, run these tests, then build".\n- **API descriptions:** OpenAPI documents are often written in YAML.\n- **Application settings** of all kinds.\n\nThis is the practical side of **infrastructure as code**: the setup of the service is a text file, kept in version control, reviewed like any code and reproducible on demand.\n\n### The famous catch\n\nYAML relies on **spaces**. One wrong indent can change the meaning or break the file. Tabs are not allowed. Many tools therefore have linters (and formatters) for YAML. When a pipeline "fails because of a YAML error" it is almost always a stray space.\n\n## Which format when?\n\n- **JSON:** exchanging data between systems via APIs.\n- **YAML:** settings and descriptions that people edit by hand.\n- **XML:** where an older standard demands it.\n- **CSV:** simple tables, exports and imports for people using spreadsheets.\n\nMany systems accept more than one and convert between them. The format is the clothing; the **meaning and quality of the data** underneath is what matters.\n\n## Reading YAML in practice\n\nYou do not need to write YAML to read it. A line `replicas: 3` says "three copies". `memory: 512Mi` says "half a gigabyte". A list of steps in a pipeline reads almost like a recipe. Asking a supplier to *show you* the configuration of an environment is a good transparency test: if it is in a clear YAML file in version control, they are working in a modern, reviewable way.\n\n::: try\nPaste the YAML example above into any online "YAML to JSON converter" (search the web). See it become the JSON version instantly. Then deliberately break it by indenting one line wrongly and watch the error. You have just seen how fragile and how readable YAML is.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nJSON, YAML, XML and CSV are just ways of writing the same facts. **JSON** for systems talking, **YAML** for humans configuring, **XML** for older standards, **CSV** for spreadsheets.\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
