INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Where should the complexity live? Databases, applications and caches', 'The design choice between putting logic in the database or in the application, and how caching makes things fast.', '## A real design question\n\nEvery system has rules and calculations: "an emergency must be attended within 4 hours", "work over a certain value needs approval". The question is: **where in the system does that logic live?** There are broadly three homes:\n\n- **In the database** as *constraints*, *triggers* and **stored procedures**. This is business logic saved and run inside the database itself.\n- **In the application** (the back end), written in a language like PHP, Java or C#.\n- **In the front end**, for convenience such as instant checks while typing. (Never the only home for a rule that matters, because users or attackers can bypass the browser.)\n\n## The case for logic in the database\n\n- **Enforced for everyone.** Whatever application or script touches the data, the rule applies. Constraints ("a repair must have a property") are best here.\n- **Speed for heavy data work.** Doing a large calculation next to the data avoids moving huge amounts across the network.\n\n## The case for moving it out\n\n- **Harder to see and test.** Logic buried in the database is often undocumented, outside version control and awkward to test. Teams find it years later and are frightened to touch it.\n- **Hard to change and to move.** Heavy use of one database\'s special features is deep **lock-in**. Moving supplier or product means rewriting everything.\n- **Scaling.** Databases are often the hardest part to scale. Putting load-heavy work in them makes the whole system slower.\n- **Different skills.** Fewer people can maintain database code than application code.\n\nA common modern approach, which you may hear described as "thin database, smart application", is: **keep the database responsible for storing data safely and enforcing simple integrity rules; put business rules in the application, where they are visible, tested and reviewable.** Neither extreme is always right, but *hidden* logic is always a risk.\n\n## Making it fast: caching\n\nWhen the same question is asked again and again, asking the database each time wastes effort. A **cache** keeps recent answers somewhere much faster (often a key-value store such as Redis, or in memory) so repeat requests are answered instantly.\n\n![A cache answers repeated requests instantly; only a miss goes to the slower database](local:caching.svg)\n\n- **Hit:** the answer is in the cache, returned immediately.\n- **Miss:** it is not, so the system asks the database, then stores the answer for next time.\n\nCaching powers most of the internet\'s speed: your browser caches images, content delivery networks cache pages near you, and applications cache database results.\n\n### The classic caveat\n\n> "There are only two hard things in computer science: cache invalidation and naming things."\n\nThe danger is **stale data**. If a repair\'s status changes but the cache still holds the old answer, someone is told something out of date. So caches have a **time to live (TTL)**, or are cleared when the data changes. Always ask: *how out of date is it allowed to be, for this data?* A list of public opening hours can be a day stale; a repair\'s live status cannot.\n\n::: try\nPress **F12**, open the **Network** tab and reload a busy site twice. On the second load many files show a size like "(memory cache)" or "(disk cache)": the browser answered from its own cache instead of downloading again. Then tick **Disable cache** at the top of the tab and reload to see the difference in load time.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nList three pieces of information in the Repairs service. For each, say how stale it could safely be: seconds, minutes or a day. This is exactly the conversation a developer needs to have with you to design caching correctly.\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
