INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Transactions and banking: how money stays safe', 'How databases and banks use all-or-nothing transactions, ACID, ledgers and idempotency keys to avoid lost or duplicated money.', '## The bank transfer problem\n\nYou transfer £100 from account A to account B. Behind the scenes, that is at least two steps: **take £100 from A**, then **add £100 to B**. What if the system fails after the first step? The money has vanished. What if you press the button twice? £200 leaves. And what if thousands of transfers are happening to the same account at once?\n\nBanks solved these problems decades ago, and the ideas apply to any system that must not lose or duplicate things, including repairs orders and payments to contractors.\n\n## Transactions: all or nothing\n\nA **transaction** groups steps so that they form a single unit: **either all succeed together or none take effect**.\n\n![A transaction takes money from one account and adds it to another; if a step fails everything is rolled back](local:transaction.svg)\n\nIf anything goes wrong midway, the database performs a **rollback**: it undoes every step so the data is exactly as before. Only when every step has worked does it **commit**, making the change permanent.\n\n## ACID: four promises\n\nReliable databases promise four properties, remembered as **ACID**:\n\n- **Atomicity:** all or nothing, as above.\n- **Consistency:** the rules always hold. A balance never goes below zero if the rules forbid it; a repair always refers to a real property.\n- **Isolation:** simultaneous transactions do not interfere with one another. Each behaves as though it were alone. (This is what protects against many of the race conditions in the previous lesson, through locking and similar techniques.)\n- **Durability:** once committed, the change survives a crash or power cut, because it is safely recorded on disk first.\n\n## Ledgers: never overwrite, only add\n\nBanks do not simply keep a number saying "balance £250". They keep a **ledger**: an **append-only** list of every credit and debit. The balance is *calculated* from the entries. Mistakes are fixed by adding a correcting entry, never by erasing the original. Why this is powerful:\n\n- A **complete audit trail**: you can always explain how you reached a figure.\n- **Nothing is lost** to a bad overwrite.\n- It suits the **finance and governance** expectations of a public body: contractor payments, credits and adjustments are best recorded as entries, not edited numbers.\n\n## Idempotency keys: pressing the button twice\n\nNetworks fail. A payment request is sent, the connection drops and the app does not know whether it worked, so it tries again. Without protection the customer is charged twice.\n\nThe solution is an **idempotency key**: the app attaches a unique label to the request ("payment-8f3a..."). If the server sees the same key again, it does not repeat the action. It simply returns the result of the first one. You met "idempotent" in the first course as "safe to repeat". The key is how a normally unsafe action, like POST, is made safe to repeat. Well-designed payment APIs insist on it.\n\n## Reconciliation and eventual consistency\n\nWhen several systems hold their own copies of the facts (for example housing, finance and a contractor\'s system), keeping them perfectly in step at every instant is hard. Many designs accept **eventual consistency**: the systems may briefly disagree, but are guaranteed to agree shortly afterwards. To catch any drift, banks run **reconciliation**: regularly comparing records between systems and investigating differences. A repairs-to-finance reconciliation, comparing completed jobs with invoices, serves the same purpose.\n\n## What to take from this\n\nYou do not need to build any of this. You need to be able to ask: *Is the money or job state handled in transactions? Are payments idempotent? Is there an audit ledger? How often do we reconcile with the contractor\'s records, and who fixes the differences?*\n\n::: try\nNext time you buy something online, press the back button after paying, or double-click the pay button (on a safe, trivial purchase or a testing site such as Stripe\'s published test mode demo). Good systems detect and refuse the second attempt. Search for "Stripe idempotent requests" and read the short explanation in their documentation. It is a very clear description of the idea.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nA contractor\'s app sends "job complete, invoice £84.50". The connection drops and the app sends it again. Describe, in plain English, what should happen so that the council pays once. Which two ideas from this lesson are involved?\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
