INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Encryption: keeping secrets on a public network', 'Keys, hashes and padlocks explained without the mathematics, and the questions to ask suppliers about protecting data.', '## Why encryption exists\n\nEverything you send across the internet passes through many other people\'s machines. **Encryption** scrambles information using a **key** so that anyone who intercepts it sees only nonsense, while the intended reader, who has the right key, can unscramble it. It is the reason an online repair report, a payment or a login can travel safely across a public network.\n\nPeople have scrambled messages for thousands of years. A famous simple method, the **Caesar cipher**, shifts every letter along by a fixed number: with a shift of 3, A becomes D. It is easy to break. Modern encryption follows the same principle (a method plus a secret key), but uses mathematics so difficult that breaking it by guessing would take longer than the age of the universe. The method is public. **Only the key is secret.**\n\n## Three tools that are often mixed up\n\n**1. Symmetric encryption: one shared key.** The same key locks and unlocks. It is very fast, and is used to protect large amounts of data (the common standard is **AES**). The difficulty: how do two parties share the key without anyone else seeing it?\n\n**2. Asymmetric (public-key) encryption: a pair of keys.** Each person has a **public key**, which they give to everyone, and a **private key**, which they never share. Anything locked with the public key can only be unlocked with the private key. Think of a **postbox**: anyone can post a letter in, but only the holder of the key can open it. It solves the sharing problem, but is slower. The same idea also gives **digital signatures**: you sign with your private key and anyone can check it with your public key, proving who sent something and that it was not altered.\n\n**3. Hashing: a one-way fingerprint.** A **hash function** turns any amount of data into a short fixed-length code. The same input always gives the same code, a tiny change gives a completely different one, and you **cannot work backwards** from the code to the original. It is used to check that a file has not been changed, and to store **passwords** safely: a good system keeps only a hash (with extra random "salt"), not the password itself. Hashing is not encryption, because it cannot be reversed.\n\n## How they work together: the padlock in your browser\n\nWhen you visit a secure site (**HTTPS**), your browser and the server use **public-key** techniques to prove who the server is and to agree a fresh shared key, then switch to fast **symmetric** encryption for the conversation. The **certificate** is the server\'s signed ID. This protocol is called **TLS**. You met the padlock in the first course. Now you know what it is doing.\n\n## Three places data needs protecting\n\n- **On the move** (in transit): while travelling across a network (TLS).\n- **In storage:** while sitting on a disk, a backup, a laptop or a phone (disk and database encryption).\n- **In use:** while being processed in memory. This is the hardest, and a growing area of research.\n\nA supplier who says "everything is encrypted" has told you almost nothing. Ask **which of the three**, and with what.\n\n## The real weak point: keys\n\nThe mathematics is rarely broken. What goes wrong is the human side: a key stored next to the data it protects, a password that is guessed, a certificate that expires and takes a service down, a laptop left on a train. **Key management**, meaning who holds the keys, how they are changed and what happens when someone leaves, matters more than the choice of algorithm.\n\nTwo other rules:\n\n- **Never invent your own encryption.** Use well-tested standards and libraries. Home-made schemes are almost always broken.\n- **Encryption does not make data safe from authorised people doing the wrong thing.** Access control and good habits are still needed.\n\n## Quantum and the future\n\nToday\'s public-key methods rely on mathematical problems that a large future quantum computer could solve. That is why new "post-quantum" methods are being introduced (see the last lesson of the first module). Symmetric encryption and hashing are much less affected.\n\n::: try\nSearch the web for an online **SHA-256 hash generator**. Type `Repair 4821` and note the long code it gives. Now type `repair 4821` (lower-case r) and compare: the result is completely different, although you changed one letter. Try to guess the original from the code. You cannot. That is a hash. (Only use made-up text, never a real password.) Then click the padlock next to the address of any secure site and look at who issued its certificate and when it expires.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nSymmetric: **one key, fast**. Asymmetric: **two keys, solves sharing and signing**. Hash: **one-way fingerprint**. The padlock uses all three, and the keys are the weak point, not the maths.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nAsk the supplier of a repairs system: "Is our data encrypted on the move, in storage and in backups? Who holds the keys, and what happens to them if you stop trading?" Write down what a good answer would sound like.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
