INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Cyber security in practice: how organisations really get hurt', 'The main kinds of attack, the layers of defence, and the practical questions for a public body and its suppliers.', '## Who is attacking, and why\n\nMost attacks on public bodies are not personal and not clever. They are **opportunistic and automated**: criminals scan the internet for weak points, send millions of messages and use whatever works. The main motives are money (stealing data or demanding a ransom), disruption, and sometimes espionage. Public sector organisations are targets because they hold personal data, run essential services and often have older systems and tight budgets.\n\nIn recent years several UK local authorities, a major national institution and, in Scotland, Comhairle nan Eilean Siar have suffered serious cyber attacks, with services disrupted for weeks or months. The common lesson was that **recovery is far harder and slower than prevention**.\n\n## The common ways in\n\n- **Phishing and social engineering.** A convincing email, text or phone call that tricks someone into clicking, sharing a password or approving a payment. The largest single cause of incidents, because it targets people, not machines.\n- **Stolen or weak passwords.** Passwords leaked from one site are tried on others (**credential stuffing**). This is why **multi-factor authentication (MFA)** matters so much.\n- **Unpatched software.** Known flaws that have a fix available but have not been applied. Attackers race to use a new flaw as soon as it is published.\n- **Ransomware.** Malicious software that encrypts an organisation\'s files and demands payment for the key, and increasingly also steals the data and threatens to publish it.\n- **Supply chain and third parties.** An attacker goes through a supplier, a contractor or a software component to reach the real target. A repairs project connects many such parties.\n- **Misconfiguration.** A storage area left open to the public, a default password never changed, a test system left running.\n- **Insiders and accidents.** Staff who misuse access, or simply send the wrong file to the wrong person.\n- **Denial of service.** Flooding a service with traffic until it cannot respond.\n\n## Defence in depth\n\nNo single protection is perfect, so good security uses **several layers**, so that when one fails another stops the attack from spreading. Think of a castle with a moat, wall, gate and guards, not just one gate.\n\n- **People:** training, a culture where it is safe to report a mistake quickly, and clear procedures for unusual payment or password requests.\n- **Identity:** MFA, strong unique passwords with a password manager, and **least privilege**, meaning people have access only to what their job needs.\n- **Devices and software:** automatic updates (patching), anti-malware, encrypted laptops, and a list of everything that is connected.\n- **Network:** segmentation, so that one infected machine cannot reach everything. A related idea is **zero trust**: do not assume anything is safe just because it is inside the network, and check every request.\n- **Data:** encryption (previous lesson), classification of what is sensitive, and strict controls on who can export it.\n- **Monitoring:** logs, alerts and someone who looks at them, so that an intruder is noticed in hours, not months.\n- **Backups:** the last line of defence against ransomware, provided at least one copy is **offline or immutable** (cannot be altered) and **restores have been tested**.\n- **Response and recovery:** a practised plan for who does what, who is told, and how services keep running manually while systems are rebuilt.\n\n## Standards and help\n\nIn the UK, the **National Cyber Security Centre (NCSC)** publishes free, practical guidance, including its "10 steps to cyber security", and runs the **Cyber Essentials** scheme (which you met in the first course) that certifies a basic set of controls. Public bodies in Scotland also work to national public-sector cyber resilience expectations. Standards such as **ISO 27001** show that an organisation manages security systematically.\n\n## What it means for the Repairs project\n\n- **Contractors and suppliers are part of the attack surface.** Ask how every party gets access, what they can reach, and how access is removed when a contract or a person leaves.\n- **Data about vulnerable people is high value and high harm.** Be clear about the minimum that really needs to be shared in each integration.\n- **Resilience, not just protection.** Assume something will eventually go wrong. How long could repairs be taken and dispatched if the main system were unavailable for a week? Is there a manual fallback?\n- **Test it.** Run a short table-top exercise: "the contractor\'s system is down and the data is encrypted: what do we do in the first hour?"\n\n::: try\nLook at the NCSC website (search "NCSC 10 steps to cyber security") and read the one-page summary of the first step. Then check your own exposure the safe way: visit **Have I Been Pwned** and enter a personal email address to see whether it appears in a known breach. If it does, check that you use a different password on every important account and turn on MFA.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nAttackers mostly take the easy route: **phishing, weak passwords, unpatched software, third parties**. Good defence is layers, practised recovery and tested backups.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nPick one contractor that has access to Repairs project data. Write down: what they can see, how they sign in, what happens to their access when a staff member leaves, and what they would do on the day they discovered a breach. Which of those four answers do you actually know?\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
SELECT @lesson, 'quick_check', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the most common way attackers get into organisations?', 0 FROM DUAL WHERE @lesson > 0;
